"""
Compliant point contact with the ground plane.

One instance per wheel or skid. The ground is the plane `y = ground_height`,
and the contact sits `radius` below `frame_a`, so a wheel is mounted at its
axle and a skid with `radius = 0`.

# Why a compliant contact rather than a constraint

The aircraft has to be able to leave the ground and come back, so the contact
cannot be a joint. A spring-damper that switches off above the ground does the
job, and the whole difficulty is making it switch off *smoothly* --- a contact
that turns on abruptly is the classic way to make a landing gear chatter, and a
damper that keeps acting as the wheel lifts will glue the aircraft down.

Both are handled here:

* The penetration uses a `tanh` gate, `d = -h*(1 - tanh(h/h_eps))/2`, which
  decays exponentially rather than algebraically above the ground. A few
  millimetres clear and the residual force is parts per million of nominal,
  so there is no phantom lift in cruise --- which a plain smooth `max(0,-h)`
  would leave behind.
* The damping term is multiplied by the penetration, so it vanishes with the
  contact instead of pulling the wheel back down as it lifts.
* The total normal force is then smoothly clamped at zero, because ground
  pushes and never pulls.

# Friction

Tangential force is `-mu * Fn * tanh(v/v_slip)` per axis, which is Coulomb
friction regularised so that it is continuous through zero sliding speed and
cannot chatter. Separate coefficients along and across the body let one
component serve both roles: a wheel gets a small `mu_x` (rolling resistance)
and a large `mu_z` (it resists sideways), while a skid gets both large.

The wheel's own rotation is not modelled. For take-off and landing rolls the
distinction does not arise; it would for wheel spin-up on touchdown or for
braking, and that is the upgrade path.
"""
component GroundContact
  "Mount; for a wheel this is the axle, for a skid the skid itself"
  frame_a = Frame3D() {^frame_a}
  """
  The wheel, drawn about its axle with the axis across the aircraft. A skid
  has `radius = 0` and would be invisible, so the drawn radius has a floor;
  what it marks is the contact point, which is the thing worth seeing.

  This is a bare `CylinderShape` driven from `frame_a`, not a
  `CylinderVisualizer` connected to it. A visualizer is a frame-connected
  component that declares `frame_a.f = 0`, and `frame_a` here is the
  component's *own* connector --- connecting anything to it makes the
  connection set force `frame_a.f` to zero, which is in direct conflict with
  this component's whole purpose of generating a contact force there. Driving
  the shape primitive directly is what `CylinderVisualizer` does internally
  anyway, and it adds no equations at all.

  The dimensions are passed as *expressions* rather than as bare parameter
  symbols because `Shape` declares `length`, `width` and `r_shape` as
  time-dependent variables, and binding one to a bare parameter puts that
  parameter and a variable in the same alias set.
  """
  wheel_vis = MultibodyComponents.CylinderShape(render = render, color = [0.15, 0.15, 0.17, 1.0], r = frame_a.r_0, R = transpose(frame_a.R), r_shape = [0.0, 0.0, -w_vis / 2], length_direction = [0.0, 0.0, 1.0], length = w_vis * 1.0, width = 2 * max(radius, 0.05), height = 2 * max(radius, 0.05))

  "Height of the ground plane"
  parameter ground_height::Length = 0.0
  "Wheel radius; zero for a skid"
  parameter radius::Length = 0.2
  "Draw the wheel"
  parameter render::Boolean = true
  "Width of the drawn wheel; purely cosmetic"
  parameter w_vis::Length = 0.09
  "Normal stiffness"
  parameter k_n::Real = 1.0e5
  """
  Normal damping, acting through the penetration. Because the damping force is
  `d_n * pen * pen_dot`, the effective damping coefficient at rest is
  `d_n * pen_static`, so compare `d_n * m*g/k_n` against the critical
  `2*sqrt(k_n*m)`. At 1e5 a 400 kg wheel sits near a third of critical, which
  absorbs a touchdown in about a second without being dead.
  """
  parameter d_n::Real = 1.0e5
  "Friction coefficient along the body x axis; small for a rolling wheel"
  parameter mu_x::Real = 0.03
  "Friction coefficient along the body z axis; large for a wheel resisting sideways"
  parameter mu_z::Real = 0.6
  "Sliding speed over which friction saturates"
  parameter v_slip::Velocity = 0.3
  "Width of the contact gate; a few millimetres"
  parameter h_eps::Length = 0.002
  """
  Smoothing on the zero clamp of the normal force. The clamp leaves `f_eps/2`
  of residual force even with no contact at all, so this has to stay small
  enough that several contacts together are still negligible against the
  aircraft weight.
  """
  parameter f_eps::Dyad.Force = 0.05

  "Height of the contact point above the ground, negative when in contact"
  variable h::Length
  "Penetration depth, zero when clear of the ground"
  variable pen::Length
  "Closing speed of the penetration"
  variable pen_dot::Velocity
  "Normal force before the zero clamp"
  variable Fn_raw::Dyad.Force
  "Normal force applied to the ground, never negative"
  variable Fn::Dyad.Force
  "Velocity of the contact point, world frame"
  variable v_c::Velocity[3]
  "Force applied at the contact, world frame"
  variable F_w::Dyad.Force[3]
relations
  h = frame_a.r_0[2] - radius - ground_height
  # tanh gate: exponentially small above the ground, exactly -h below it
  pen = -h * 0.5 * (1 - tanh(h / h_eps))
  pen_dot = der(pen)
  v_c = der(frame_a.r_0)

  # Damping scaled by the penetration so it releases with the contact
  Fn_raw = pen * (k_n + d_n * pen_dot)
  Fn = 0.5 * (Fn_raw + sqrt(Fn_raw^2 + f_eps^2))

  F_w = [-mu_x * Fn * tanh(v_c[1] / v_slip),
         Fn,
         -mu_z * Fn * tanh(v_c[3] / v_slip)]
  frame_a.f = -MultibodyComponents.resolve2(frame_a.R, F_w)
  frame_a.tau = [0, 0, 0]
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://Multtest/GroundContact.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "frame_a": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": 450, "y1": -50, "x2": 550, "y2": 50}}}
    }
  }
}
end

"""
A single wheel carrying a mass, dropped onto the ground.

The mass must settle at the static deflection `m*g/k_n` and stop there; it is
the check that the contact both supports the weight and releases cleanly rather
than bouncing forever or sticking.
"""
test component TestGroundContactDrop
  world = MultibodyComponents.World() {^world}
  body = MultibodyComponents.Body(m = 400.0,
                                  orientation_state = MultibodyComponents.OrientationState.Quaternion(),
                                  statePriority = 1000, linearStatePriority = 1000,
                                  r_0(initial = [0.0, 0.3, 0.0]), v_0(initial = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0])) {^body}
  contact = GroundContact(radius = 0.2, k_n = 1.0e5, d_n = 2.0e4) {^contact}
relations
  connect(body.frame_a, contact.frame_a) {^id1}
metadata {
  "_links": {
    "world": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 60, "y1": 760, "x2": 160, "y2": 860}}}},
    "body": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 450, "y1": 200, "x2": 550, "y2": 300}}}},
    "contact": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 450, "y1": 520, "x2": 550, "y2": 620}}}},
    "id1": {"Dyad": {"edges": [{"S": 1, "M": [], "E": 2}]}}
  }
}
end
